## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code uses `Thread.Abort()` to kill a thread exceeding a deadline. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Block until completion or timeout; throws on cancellation
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### If `work` must cooperate with cancellation (recommended):

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;

    try
    {
        var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);
        return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

Callers then check the token inside their work:

```csharp
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // cooperative checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return result;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, the token won't interrupt a blocking call (e.g., `Thread.Sleep`, synchronous I/O). The work must check `token.IsCancellationRequested` or call async APIs that accept tokens. |
| **`WaitAsync` unblocks the caller but doesn't stop the work** | If `work` ignores the token, it keeps running in the background after timeout. This is the fundamental trade-off of cooperative cancellation. |
| **Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks internal timers. Always wrap in `using`. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at the top level to convert to `TimeoutException`. Let it propagate elsewhere. |
| **Uncooperative code (can't modify `work`)** | If you truly can't add token checks, move the work to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()` — that's the only reliable way to forcibly stop uncooperative code in modern .NET. |

---

### What was removed

| .NET Framework API | Replacement |
|--------------------|-------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` (via timeout) |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed — not needed with cooperative model |
| Manual `Thread` + `Join` | `Task.Run` + `WaitAsync` |
